In article <1648@sunic.sunet.se> eric@sunic.sunet.se (Eric Thomas SUNET) writes:
>Most cats will fetch a ball of paper or something similar when they are in a
>playing mood, my point was that I have never seen a cat bring the newspaper
>(such cats probably do exist, but they're not common) or anything you didn't
>throw in the first place, and I've never seen a cat fetch anything when it
>isn't in the mood to fetch things anyway :-) Also, sometimes the cat will dash
>after the thrown object as if it was a piece of the most delicious food it has
>ever tasted, grab it, run back to you and stop about halfways, dropping the
>object and itself onto the floor and casting an amused look at you ("now it's
>your turn to come and get it") :-)

Well, my cat (real Vanilla) must be obsessive on the subject; I wake up in the
morning with her standing on my chest, her favorite paper ball in her mouth.

I have, of course, seen the behavior you describe.  She has me very well
trained at fetching to _her_!  Also at filling her food dish, etc.

My dungeon cat (virtual Vanilla) is much more reliable as a fetcher, but she
has a nasty bitey habit, which the real Vanilla has long given up as a sure
source of pops on the head.  Virtual Vanilla also has the nice habit of
puffing up to catamount size as the game goes on, while real Vanilla is as
big as she's going to get, and spayed to boot, a trick I would never dare
try on virtual Vanilla.

